<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Tsangpa dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1565 CE – 1642 CE · capital Samdrubtse (Shigatse)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kings of Tsang who briefly united central Tibet in the early 17th century, before falling to the Gelug school and its Mongol allies in 1642.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhingshak-tseten-dorje">Zhingshak Tseten Dorje</a>, an official of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rinpungpa">Rinpungpa</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shigatse-dzong">Samdrubtse</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shigatse">Shigatse</a>), rebelled and by 1565 had replaced his masters as ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsang">Tsang</a>. His descendants expanded steadily, and in 1618 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karma-phuntsok-namgyal">Karma Phuntsok Namgyal</a> conquered Ü, ending the last <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/phagmodrupa-dynasty">Phagmodrupa dynasty</a> influence and ruling most of central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Tsangpa kings, who ruled from the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shigatse-dzong">Samdrubtse</a>, were patrons of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karma-kagyu">Karma Kagyu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jonang">Jonang</a> schools and hostile to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a>, whose monasteries had powerful Mongol supporters. They issued a law code and tried to centralise the country. Wars with the new state of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zhabdrung-bhutan">Zhabdrung Bhutan</a> to the south were costly.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> leaders invited the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/khoshut-khanate">Khoshut</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gushi-khan">Güshi Khan</a>, who defeated the Tsangpa allies and in 1642 took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shigatse-dzong">Samdrubtse</a>; the last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karma-tenkyong-wangpo">Karma Tenkyong Wangpo</a>, was executed. Güshi Khan handed authority to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">Fifth Dalai Lama</a>, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ganden-phodrang">Ganden Phodrang</a> and the Khoshut Khanate.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tsangpa-dynasty.jpg" alt="Tsangpa dynasty" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: René Heise · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lhasa_Potala.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1565 CE – 1642 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Samdrubtse (Shigatse)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Zhingshak Tseten Dorje, Karma Phuntsok Namgyal, Karma Tenkyong Wangpo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tibetan Buddhism (Karma Kagyu)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tibetan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tsangpa, Kings of Tsang</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1604&amp;focus=tsangpa-dynasty" aria-label="Tsangpa dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7392 967 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tsangpa dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7393 1364h0M7317 1364h0M7372 1351h0M7228 1376h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7712 1223h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7712 1223h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day China. <a href="/atlas?year=1604&amp;focus=tsangpa-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
